    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sine is helpful for finding heights of things moving in a circular motion. The minute hand on the Elizabeth Tower, a famous clock in London whose bell is nicknamed Big Ben, extends 11.5 feet to the edge of the 23 foot diameter clock face.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What can we say about the height of the end of the minute hand at other times? Let’s start by imagining a unit circle centered on the clock.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "At 12:10 the minute hand makes a \\(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) radian angle together with the ray through the 3 on the clock. Using our unit circle, we know \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0.5\\). For a clock that has a radius of 1, the height of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another question we can ask is: when is the end of the minute hand 174.25 feet above the ground? Since \\(174.25 = 180 - 5.75\\), this means that the tip of the minute hand is 5.75 feet below the center of the clock, and this is…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The identity \\(\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x) = 1\\) relating the sine and cosine of a number. It is called the Pythagorean identity because it follows from the Pythagorean theorem.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Pythagorean identity",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The length of an interval at which a periodic function repeats. A function \\(f\\) has a period, \\(p\\), if \\(f(x+p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L07-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: Around a Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The circle in the coordinate plane with radius 1 and center the origin.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
